% ============================================================================
% subordinating-conjunction-relationship-type — each named kind of
% relationship a subordinating conjunction can signal between its clauses,
% decoded from a span already sitting unused inside `conjunction-type.adj`'s
% own already-quoted source sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `conjunction-type.adj`
% (`conjunction_type(type, description)`, coordinating_conjunction/
% correlative_conjunction/subordinating_conjunction → their own defining
% sentences): that table's own header already reproduces, verbatim, the
% Grammarly sentence for the subordinating conjunction in full --
%
%     "Subordinating conjunctions join dependent clauses to the
%      independent clauses of sentences, signaling cause and effect,
%      comparison, contrast, time, or some other kind of relationship
%      between the clauses."
%
% -- but the table's `type, description` schema only had room for WHAT a
% subordinating conjunction does structurally (joining clauses), not the
% FOUR named relationship kinds the same sentence lists it can signal. New
% `subordinating_conjunction_relationship_type(relationship_type,
% conjunction_type)` table decodes those four named kinds as their own
% rows:
%
%   cause_and_effect → subordinating_conjunction
%   comparison        → subordinating_conjunction
%   contrast          → subordinating_conjunction
%   time              → subordinating_conjunction
%
% The sentence's own trailing "or some other kind of relationship between
% the clauses" is deliberately excluded -- it names no additional
% relationship, only gestures at an open-ended remainder, so tabling it
% would invent a category the source never actually names.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? subordinating_conjunction_relationship_type(cause_and_effect, $C)
%       % subordinating_conjunction
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `subordinating_conjunction_relationship_type(relationship_type,
% conjunction_type)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the conjunction type
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% relationship type that is not one of these four (it never invents a
% row). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a conjunction type and recall
% every relationship type it signals:
%
%     ? subordinating_conjunction_relationship_type($R, subordinating_conjunction)
%       % cause_and_effect, comparison, contrast, time
%
% A recall on a relationship type this exact sentence does NOT name --
% `concession` (a real relationship subordinating conjunctions like
% "although" can express in general grammar, but not one of the four this
% specific already-quoted sentence lists) -- correctly abstains rather
% than inventing a row for it. A recall on a DIFFERENT, real conjunction
% category this stdlib already tables -- `coordinating_conjunction` (the
% already-shipped sibling row in `conjunction-type.adj` itself) -- also
% correctly abstains, since this table's only conjunction_type value is
% subordinating_conjunction.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted
% inside `conjunction-type.adj`'s own header (its truth table, "quote
% (verbatim)" column, subordinating_conjunction row), from the SAME
% Grammarly "Conjunctions" article that table already cites -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of that
% already-verified quote. `trust consensus` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (Grammarly is a reputable secondary reference,
% not a primary standards body).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table subordinating_conjunction_relationship_type {
    columns relationship_type, conjunction_type

    row (cause_and_effect, subordinating_conjunction)
    row (comparison, subordinating_conjunction)
    row (contrast, subordinating_conjunction)
    row (time, subordinating_conjunction)

    source "Subordinating conjunctions join dependent clauses to the independent clauses of sentences, signaling cause and effect, comparison, contrast, time, or some other kind of relationship between the clauses."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/conjunctions/"
    trust consensus
}
